+- **Initial manifest and stable review root:** Create the versioned initial manifest when the review session is created, before opening its first reviewer turn. When that manifest is completed/sealed, capture the subject repository's then-current full HEAD as `review_root_commit`, together with repository/branch identity, creation time and the submitted FUR revision/digest. Verify HEAD and submitted bytes remain stable across capture; retry or report unavailable context rather than writing an inconsistent root. The SHA is the HEAD observed while completing the manifest, not the future commit that will contain it; no self-referential commit is required. Preserve the immutable initial manifest as collateral, with later records linked to its identity/digest. This is a repository-context anchor, not a retained initial FUR snapshot or cumulative patch baseline. Preserve the earliest retained FUR's original writing-basis commit through its lineage metadata, separately from the session root; if the FUR was written before session creation, those SHAs may differ. Historical missing origin metadata remains unavailable.
+- **Drift at acceptance and code-context forks:** Compare `review_root_commit` with the observed acceptance HEAD and inspect relevant staged, unstaged and untracked changes. The versioned manifest defines the exact FUR and declared review-collateral paths allowed for this comparison; directory-wide exclusions must not conceal application source, configuration, tests or dependency changes. Record the compared SHAs, changed paths and classification. If the only net changes are the FUR and declared collateral, keep the same review root and record `review-only` drift; ordinary publication commits do not create a new code basis. If other files differ, retain the original root and append a linked code-context divergence record containing the observed HEAD, affected paths and any observed merge/rebase context. This is an informational fork of the specification's code context, not a command to create a Git branch, reject an otherwise valid participant decision, or rewrite the old root. Record non-ancestor/history-rewritten cases even when endpoint code trees are equivalent; ancestry changes alone do not prove code changed. Distinguish observed endpoint content differences from any stronger claim about every intervening commit. Unresolvable roots or incomplete comparisons are `unavailable`, never assumed review-only. These records do not waive the within-turn stale HEAD/FUR checks below; drift between sealed turns and historical acceptance-to-current drift have different timing from a change during an open turn.
